# an assignment asks the objects above, and a name there that has become read only refuses it: `Object.freeze(Object.prototype)`, which anybody may do at any time. Outside strict code nothing is thrown.
# So `made.valueOf = 1` fills no field, and a read of it may find the function above. A definition asks nobody: a literal that lists the name has it. What is assigned behind a literal fills no field of a record, under whatever name: the literal is the store of "filled"
# function assigned() { const made = {}; made.valueOf = 1; made.size = 2; return made; }   function listed() { return { valueOf: 1, size: 2 }; }
